 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** The server publishes committed revisions over persistent socket sessions, owning connection capacity and replay; browsers own reconnect and catch-up. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its lag and repeated reads. A hosted realtime service was rejected for recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosting accepts the connection and reconnect operations burden to get fast updates without a realtime vendor. See [websocket-delivery](decisions/websocket-delivery.md).
